{
  "about": "Shared test vectors for the SeeForMe voice-command parser. Python (seeforme/live/voice.py) and the web app (web/src/voice.ts) must both pass every case. 'wake' cases are hands-free: the wake phrase ('see for me' and its mis-hearings) is required and the command is the text after the last wake phrase. 'direct' cases are push-to-talk or typed: the wake phrase is optional. intent null means: ignore the utterance. Arguments are lowercase. A find target has no leading 'me' ('find me a chair'), no 'the way to', articles or 'nearest'/'closest', and spoken numbers as digits; 'the way out' is 'exit'. flip has arg 'front' or 'back' when the phrase names a camera side ('front camera', 'selfie camera' -> front; 'back camera', 'rear camera', 'switch to the back camera' -> back) and null when it does not ('flip camera', 'switch camera', 'other camera'). New phrasings go to 'direct' (the wake phrase is optional there, so 'see for me ...' forms fit too) or 'unicode', never to 'wake'. 'unicode' cases (mode 'wake' or 'direct' each) hold non-ASCII text: both parsers use Unicode words (Python's \\w: letters, numbers, '_'), word boundaries and decimal digits, and NFC first. They are kept apart from 'wake' because scripts/eval_voice.py synthesises the 'wake' cases.",
